From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 03:38:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11186/11362] Memory index drift: a check that fires, not prose
 (REQ-INFRA-6975)

A memory file's MEMORY.md line and description: are what a session reads.
The body is what nobody reads until something goes wrong. Twice on
2026-09-05 a body was appended to while its summary kept describing the
old contents, and the stale summary was believed. The prose rule existed
(feedback_write_it_down_or_lose_it) and failed within seven hours.

What this adds:
- scripts/memory_index_drift.py: a baseline sidecar in the memory
  directory (outside git, so no history exists), maintained by
  OBSERVATION: when the summary moves the baseline follows; when only
  the body grows (>=2 non-blank lines) the file is flagged and held until
  BOTH description and index line move. No discipline is needed to keep
  the sidecar current, which is the difference from the rule that failed.
- Dashboard: an always-printed `memory` line naming the population and
  any DRIFTED files. Fails closed and loud: UNREADABLE / RESET / baseline
  created, never an empty result.
- PostToolUse hook (Write|Edit): a stateless reminder at the moment of
  the append, from the edit payload. Read-only, exit 0, never blocks.
  Quiet once the description has moved since baseline.

Rejected: description-vs-index word overlap (median Jaccard 0.23,
52/152 files below 0.15 -- noise); bolded-heading counts (fires on every
healthy long file); per-section token coverage (a summary cannot name a
token from each of 14 sections); a helper script (discipline again).

Measured: on the 32 real logged body edits the hook rule fires on 29,
all substantive appends, 0 touch-ups. Historical replay of the dashboard
rule was attempted; the session logs reconstruct 0 of 35 files to disk,
so they are an incomplete edit history and yield no rate. The live rate
is measurable from the next hourly run onward.

Mutation proofs: 9/9 RED with byte-identical restores, including the
dashboard call site (M3) and the fail-closed paths (M6, M7, M8).

